## 14. Process Explained With Theory

### Training vs Evaluation in This Thesis

This thesis is not mainly a deep neural network training thesis. Most strong methods are unsupervised or lightweight selection methods using precomputed embeddings. The balanced selector uses machine learning at the method-selection level: it learns from 29 videos which method may work best for the held-out video.

Evidence: `scripts/method_selector_experiment.py`

### Real-Life Analogy

Imagine you have 30 lecture notebooks. For each notebook, several students suggest where chapter breaks should be. The selector learns from 29 notebooks which student tends to be reliable for certain kinds of lectures, then chooses one student for the 30th notebook. That is similar to the LOO selector.

### Important Process Steps

| Step | Theory | Example from project | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| Convert audio to text | ASR recognizes spoken words | Whisper transcripts | `data/transcripts/` |
| Sentence timeline | Segmentation needs ordered units | sentences with start/end times | `data/sentences/` |
| Meaning vectors | Embeddings place similar meaning nearby | BGE/E5 `.npy` files | `data/embeddings/` |
| Gap scoring | Topic change causes semantic dissimilarity | cosine/dissimilarity gap | `src/lecseg/models/fusion.py` |
| Boundary selection | Choose strongest plausible gaps | divisive/cross-model/selector | `scripts/run_eval.py` |
| Evaluation | Compare predicted and true boundaries | Pk/WD/BS/F1 | `src/lecseg/metrics.py` |

