## 29. Strong Defense Narrative

This thesis builds LECSEG, a reproducible low-resource system and benchmark for lecture-video topic segmentation. The main goal is to automatically identify where topics change in long lecture videos, so students can navigate lectures more easily.

The work includes a real 30-video dataset, transcripts, sentence-level timelines, chapter ground truth, reviewed subtopics, multiple embedding models, preprocessing code, segmentation methods, evaluation scripts, statistical tests, and defense materials. The best local deployable operating point is a balanced leave-one-video-out selector with Pk 0.3588 and WindowDiff 0.3739. This improves over the stable BGE-divisive baseline, while the thesis honestly reports that the gain over the cross-model method is not statistically significant for Pk/WD.

The contribution is not external state of the art. The contribution is a careful benchmark-and-diagnosis artifact that shows the main bottleneck is robust boundary selection.

### 29.1 Short Confident Version

My thesis builds a reproducible lecture segmentation benchmark and pipeline. On 30 public lectures, I tested many methods and found that a balanced method selector achieves the best local result, Pk 0.3588 and WD 0.3739. The result improves over the stable baseline, but the thesis is honest that exact boundary detection and domain generalization remain open problems.

### 29.2 Detailed Confident Version

The thesis starts from a real problem: long lecture videos are hard to navigate. I created LECSEG-30, a 30-video benchmark with 32.52 hours of lectures across five domains. The data includes YouTube chapter boundaries and reviewed subtopic labels. The system transcribes lectures, splits them into sentences, creates sentence embeddings, detects semantic changes, and evaluates predicted topic boundaries with Pk, WindowDiff, Boundary Similarity, and F1.

Several methods were compared, including classical baselines, embedding baselines, BGE-divisive segmentation, cross-model selection, multimodal diagnostics, TreeSeg-style comparison, and a leave-one-video-out selector. The balanced selector gives the best mean Pk/WD result. The thesis also reports failures, such as poor transfer from BERT-wiki and over-segmentation from prosody and BERTopic. The main finding is that candidate generation is not the only problem; the hard problem is selecting the right boundaries.

### 29.3 If the Panel Challenges the Result

"The result is modest, and I agree it does not solve the problem completely. But it is statistically supported over the baseline and the limitations are measured. The thesis contribution is the benchmark, reproducible evaluation, and diagnosis of the remaining bottleneck."

### 29.4 If the Panel Says "The Result Is Not Good Enough"

"For a large production system, more data and stronger supervised models would be needed. But for an undergraduate low-resource research thesis, the work is defendable because it builds the dataset, implements multiple methods, evaluates them rigorously, and identifies why performance plateaus."

### 29.5 If the Panel Asks "What Is Your Contribution?"

"The contribution is LECSEG-30, a compact hierarchical lecture segmentation benchmark, plus a reproducible pipeline and empirical analysis showing which lightweight methods work, which fail, and why boundary selection remains the key unsolved problem."

