## 35. Final Evidence-Based Conclusion

This thesis built LECSEG, a reproducible low-resource lecture-video topic segmentation artifact. The project used 30 public YouTube lectures, 32.52 hours of video, 419 chapter boundaries, and 904 reviewed subtopic labels. It implemented preprocessing, sentence splitting, embeddings, visual/OCR/prosody features, segmentation baselines, proposed models, evaluation metrics, result analysis, tables, figures, thesis report, paper draft, and defense materials.

The strongest stable baseline was BGE-divisive segmentation with Pk 0.3884 and WD 0.3956. The best single global method was cross-model conservative selection with Pk 0.3713 and WD 0.3764. The best deployable local operating point was the balanced leave-one-video-out selector with Pk 0.3588, WD 0.3739, and F1@2 0.0893. Negative results showed that several signals, including prosody, BERTopic, discourse markers, GPT-2 perplexity, and out-of-domain BERT-wiki segmentation, do not reliably solve chapter-level lecture segmentation.

The thesis is strongest as a benchmark-and-diagnosis contribution. It is not an external state-of-the-art claim. It is defendable because it provides real data, real code, real experiments, statistical tests, negative evidence, and a clear future direction: robust boundary selection using more data and stronger candidate ranking.

What remains incomplete: final demo verification, public release cleanup, stronger domain-general selector, stronger exact boundary F1, and larger dataset expansion.

Overall strength: defendable undergraduate thesis, especially if presented honestly as low-resource reproducible research rather than exaggerated leaderboard performance.

